blocks:
  - type: metin
    metin: |-
      Why has getting out of bed on a Monday become so hard? Why does the job you once enjoyed now leave you empty? Why doesn't your tiredness lift, even after a weekend of rest?

      These questions may share a single name: burnout. And telling it apart from everyday work stress is the first step toward doing something about it — because the two call for different responses. Stress usually eases when you reduce the load; burnout often needs a deeper kind of repair.
  - type: metin
    baslik: What is burnout?
    metin: |-
      The term "burnout" was first used in 1974 by psychologist Herbert Freudenberger to describe workers who were "burning themselves out" under relentless demands. Today the most widely used definition comes from the World Health Organization (WHO).

      In the WHO's ICD-11 classification, burnout is defined as a syndrome resulting from **chronic workplace stress that has not been successfully managed**. One detail matters a great deal: the WHO classifies burnout not as a medical condition but as an **occupational phenomenon** — meaning its source is usually not personal "weakness" but unsustainable working conditions.

      That means you don't have to look for the fault solely in yourself. But it also means that recovering from burnout involves addressing both your conditions and your relationship with yourself.
  - type: kart-grid
    baslik: 'Work stress or burnout? Here''s the core difference'
    giris: 'Stress and burnout overlap, but they aren''t the same thing. Put crudely: stress is a state of "too much," while burnout is a state of "not enough." You can spot the difference like this:'
    kolon: 2
    kartlar:
      - baslik: Work stress
        maddeler:
          - Over-engagement, system stuck "on"
          - Emotions intense and over-reactive
          - A sense of urgency and hyperactivity
          - Loss of energy — but you still "care"
          - Anxiety takes the lead
          - Rest brings partial recovery
      - baslik: Burnout
        maddeler:
          - Disengagement, system stuck "off"
          - Emotions blunted, distant, indifferent
          - A sense of helplessness and hopelessness
          - Loss of motivation, ideals and hope
          - Detachment and emotional numbness take the lead
          - Rest often isn't enough
  - type: kart-yigini
    baslik: The three dimensions of burnout
    giris: 'Psychologist Christina Maslach''s model — the most widely accepted in the field — defines burnout not as a single "tiredness" but as three dimensions that feed one another:'
    kartlar:
      - baslik: 1. Exhaustion (emotional depletion)
        metin: The feeling of hitting rock bottom on energy, drained both physically and emotionally. Waking up already tired, and finding the smallest task looming like a mountain, are typical signs of this dimension.
      - baslik: 2. Distance (cynicism / detachment)
        metin: A coldness, cynicism and emotional withdrawal toward your work, clients or colleagues. Things you once cared about no longer move you; the thought "why bother, what difference does it make" circles your mind.
      - baslik: 3. Ineffectiveness (reduced sense of accomplishment)
        metin: The sense that whatever you do, you fall short and can't get anything right. Even when your performance holds steady, you feel like a failure — which erodes confidence and motivation further still.
  - type: kart-grid
    baslik: What are the symptoms of burnout?
    giris: 'Burnout is more than "low morale"; it leaves concrete marks on the body, emotions and behaviour.'
    kolon: 3
    kartlar:
      - baslik: Physical
        maddeler:
          - Persistent fatigue, low energy
          - Sleep problems
          - Headaches and muscle pain
          - Getting ill often, weakened immunity
          - Changes in appetite and digestion
      - baslik: Emotional
        maddeler:
          - Loss of motivation and hope
          - Cynicism, indifference
          - Helplessness, feeling trapped
          - Irritability and low tolerance
          - A sense of failure and inadequacy
      - baslik: Behavioural
        maddeler:
          - Avoiding work and people
          - Putting off responsibilities
          - Difficulty concentrating
          - Dropping performance
          - Escaping into alcohol, caffeine or screens
  - type: numarali-liste
    baslik: Could you be burnt out? Ask yourself 5 questions
    giris: These questions won't give you a clinical diagnosis, but they can help you judge whether it's time to pause and seek support.
    stil: acik
    maddeler:
      - Do you feel unrested even after a weekend or a holiday?
      - Are you feeling increasingly cynical, distant or indifferent toward your work?
      - Do tasks you once did with ease now feel overwhelming?
      - Does a sense that you're never good enough follow you, whatever you do?
      - Have physical symptoms like fatigue, insomnia or aches persisted for months?
    kapanis: If you answered "yes" to three or more, what you're experiencing may be more than ordinary tiredness.
  - type: bilgi-kutusu
    ton: yesil
    baslik: Burnout or depression?
    metin: The two can overlap, and sometimes occur together, but they aren't the same. Burnout is largely tied to work and working conditions; it often eases somewhat once you step away from the job context. [Depression](/en/blog/what-is-depression.html), by contrast, spreads across every area of life, and time off won't fix it. Because prolonged burnout can pave the way for depression, it's important to clarify the distinction with a professional.
  - type: adimlar
    baslik: How do you recover from burnout?
    giris: 'You don''t recover from burnout by "trying harder" — trying too hard is usually the root of the problem. Recovery tends to follow a path something like this:'
    adimlar:
      - baslik: Name what's happening
        metin: Saying "I'm not lazy, I'm burnt out" may seem small, but it's decisive. Seeing your experience as a response to unsustainable conditions rather than a personal flaw breaks the cycle of self-blame.
      - baslik: Genuinely reduce the load
        metin: Setting boundaries, saying "no," delegating what you can. Even a short break makes room for recovery. Piling on new demands while your energy tank is empty only deepens burnout.
      - baslik: Repair the body
        metin: Sleep, regular movement and real rest aren't luxuries; they're the physiological foundation of healing. Mental recovery can't begin until the nervous system comes down from chronic alarm.
      - baslik: Reconnect and rebuild meaning
        metin: Burnout isolates people. Contact with those you trust, activities that do you good, and rethinking what your work means to you slowly bring blunted emotions back to life.
      - baslik: Seek professional support
        metin: 'Working with a psychologist helps you notice the thought and behaviour patterns that feed burnout and build sustainable boundaries. [Cognitive behavioural therapy](/en/blog/what-is-anxiety-disorder.html) is one of the most commonly used approaches in this process.'
  - type: bilgi-kutusu
    ton: turuncu
    baslik: When should you seek professional support?
    metin: If symptoms have persisted for months, spilled into your life beyond work, disrupted your sleep and physical health, or the feeling of "I can't go on like this" has become permanent, it's worth speaking with a professional. Early intervention helps prevent burnout from turning into depression.
  - type: metin
    baslik: 'In short: tiredness lifts with rest, burnout asks you to change course'
    metin: |-
      Work stress is part of life and, most of the time, manageable. Burnout is a warning sign — your body and mind saying "this pace can't be sustained." The good news is that burnout, with the right support, is reversible.

      If you recognised yourself in this article, your first step doesn't have to be a big one. If you're unsure where to start, our guide on [when to seek therapy](/en/blog/when-to-go-to-therapy.html) can point the way; and if you live outside Famagusta, [online therapy](/en/blog/online-therapy-north-cyprus.html) is an option too. In a first session we assess your situation together and map out the path that suits you.
